---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout title="Goodreads Book Review - Rabiah Khanum">
  <div class="min-h-screen bg-gradient-to-br from-slate-50 via-white to-purple-50">
    <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-6 py-16">
      
      <!-- Header -->
      <div class="text-center mb-12">
        <a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}writing`} class="inline-flex items-center text-yellow-700 hover:text-yellow-900 mb-6 transition-colors">
          <svg class="w-5 h-5 mr-2" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
            <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M10 19l-7-7m0 0l7-7m-7 7h18" />
          </svg>
          Back to Writing
        </a>
        
        <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-4" style="font-family: 'Playfair Display', serif;">
          My First Detailed Book Review
        </h1>
        
        <p class="text-lg text-slate-600 max-w-2xl mx-auto">
          An emotional yet critical review of Take it Back by Kia Abdullah
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Story Content -->
      <article class="bg-white rounded-2xl shadow-lg p-8 md:p-12 mb-12 border border-slate-100">
        <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none">
          <p class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed text-justify">
            First, I want to make this clear: this story is as complex as it should be, and the author did a fair job of depicting the true complexity of race, religion, and culture in this specific setting. Monsters are not bound by any culture, religion, or race. We cannot deny that upbringing plays a role in creating those monsters, but society and expectations play a far larger role, as is definitively clear in the portrayal of the Muslim boys in this book. But I still believe that to blame only culture and upbringing without taking into account the surroundings in which people are raised is an unfair choice. All in all, the complexity of the chosen topics in this book and the sinful, cruel world we live in is a subject we all need to ponder more broadly.
          </p>

          <p class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed">
            Now I will state my actual feelings towards certain topics discussed in the book.
          </p>
          
          <p class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed text-justify">
            <em>'Can't you see, Mum? Oppression doesn't spread through men with guns, or bombs on trains. Oppression spreads when women like you tell their daughters to marry a certain man, or wear a certain dress, or work a certain job. It happens when women like you tell us – gently and with all the love in the world – not to peek above the parapet, instead to stay at home, to be quiet, to be kind, to be good.'</em>
          </p>

          <p class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed text-justify">
            What I have to say may come across as a bit too hateful, but I am simply disappointed. But this — this is the line that gives me the ick. Brown Muslim people born and raised in the west still think they are so far above the culture back home. But it is not so black and white as they portray. I believe this version is not entirely a truth but rather a depiction made palatable for the western audience. When thinking about Islam and the people who follow it — those who also have their culture deeply entwined with it — people often forget which is which, and I personally think that is the root of all problems. Humans are so bound by their need for conformity that they always mould themselves to fit better into wherever they live, even if they don't want to. And that is what these mothers are doing — trying to protect their children from the ire of their culture, because that is what a mother does: always shield her children with whatever little power she has. If she cannot stand up the way emboldened children of a new era often demand, it is because she was not dealt the same cards as them.
          </p>

          <p class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed text-justify">
            Islam and its rules have always tried to nurture and protect society in a way that could yield the fruits we can only imagine in a utopia, but the barrier that culture and tradition erect is what hinders that result. Human nature — its sinfulness, its hunger for all that life has to offer, often at the expense of others, and its compulsion to conform to societal norms — is what actual oppression looks like. If asking your children to dress a certain way, to cover themselves, is so oppressive, then why can't the opposite also hold true? Is the western societal pressure that pushes people — in the name of freedom and choice — to uncover, to drink, to party, even when they do not want to, not equally oppressive? Is making them believe that this is freedom, forcing an ideology onto the unwilling, not oppressive as well? If marrying and becoming a homemaker is oppressive, then why is surrendering oneself freely to all, simply to be labelled confident, not oppressive too? We are all trying to fit into certain narratives to appease others, and I do not blame religion for that — I blame culture, whether it belongs to the west or the east.
          </p>

          <p class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed text-justify">
            Women's rights have always been a struggle, and women have always been diminished in the cultures of both the east and the west. So to condemn a single way of living is an injustice, to say the least. You cannot mock one way of life when your own is tarnished beyond repair. I do understand the sentiment the author is coming from — the character has witnessed injustice through the singular lens of being born in the west to a brown culture. I, too, have seen life through a different lens, having been born into a brown culture in the east and given certain privileges, such as education, employment, and living independently. And yes, I call them privileges, because I could have been born into a far crueller position, as none of it was within my control. I have faced the barriers this culture imposes, yet I cannot bring myself to mock the rules of the religion I follow, knowing full well that we are far from perfectly adhering to it. To call it unfair feels unfair in itself, when I live in a society that cannot even comprehend oppression, let alone dismantle or resist it.
          </p>

          <p class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed text-justify">
            Humans have always been cruel — across every culture and every continent — and that cruelty existed long before Islam. Islam came to offer a way out, if one chose to follow it. But as with all religions, it was eventually diluted to the point where everyone picks what is convenient and then wonders why those who preach it differ so greatly from what they preach. The western world has never genuinely tried to understand it, and the relentless negative portrayals — spotlighting everything that is wrong while glossing over the good — do nothing to help. A religion cannot right all the wrongs of a society that refuses to accept it wholly, no matter how peaceful it may be.
          </p>
          
        </div>
      </article>

      <!-- Back Button -->
      <div class="text-center">
        <a 
          href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}writing`}
          class="inline-flex items-center px-6 py-3 bg-gradient-to-r from-yellow-500 to-yellow-700 text-white rounded-lg font-medium hover:shadow-lg transition-all duration-300 hover:scale-105"
        >
          <svg class="w-5 h-5 mr-2" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
            <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M10 19l-7-7m0 0l7-7m-7 7h18" />
          </svg>
          Back to Writing
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  article {
    animation: fadeInUp 0.6s ease-out;
  }

  @keyframes fadeInUp {
    from {
      opacity: 0;
      transform: translateY(20px);
    }
    to {
      opacity: 1;
      transform: translateY(0);
    }
  }
</style>
